#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23567a86-8e1e-5b94-bac6-09c79cdb42c9" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19197229-5d48-5afc-abfd-66d923c6020f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520820} {:answer/id #uuid "39d9a121-2cea-54a4-be8c-879ad88faecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520891} {:answer/id #uuid "8646dd0f-8b2b-546d-837a-0eb10eef94e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521070} {:answer/id #uuid "da80b901-76be-5b81-adc9-6cd165130508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521252} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf35fc4-0777-595a-b312-adcad1105f1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebf35fc4-0777-595a-b312-adcad1105f1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521287} :answer-field/id #uuid "9379bdb2-a24f-536a-8f21-7af1b41bfabf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d82dc916-4a3b-544e-8c9e-9820eeb6151d" :question/math-academy-id "q-100057" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6dd62794ee072530cd2b6c8028101b58ae617d6757d29f79de90d1a439f6529b.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(6x-1)f(x)}{5}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." :question/worked-solution "We find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=2.$ So we calculate\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(6x-1)f(x)}{5} & =\\frac{1}{5}(6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 1}x-1)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(6(1)-1)(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100953 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0159149-010c-5bef-a28d-85c4c43f0586" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4660fdd8-5261-5c4e-bc67-7363b74699d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520920} {:answer/id #uuid "71617208-0858-502e-bb7b-40761d03d4d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521018} {:answer/id #uuid "90bb2958-b97e-5235-b2bd-23dc8aa76027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521093} {:answer/id #uuid "a2416292-adfa-5246-a3d7-f18168238265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521128} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd4397a-616a-58ad-bf05-1dd4260c9cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90bb2958-b97e-5235-b2bd-23dc8aa76027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521093} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a6654db-527e-57c6-a150-229af646b191" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a5e8422b-8be3-5e23-8b9e-af4b866bbaf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-100100" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3cc4f4447ee6fb212222b28c7e4c31cca435c542e3d2f25ae3f3331a3b98da2b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x).$ Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{f(x)+2g(x)}{1+xg(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{f(x)+2g(x)}{1+xg(x)}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)+2g(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(1+xg(x))}.\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=6$  and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=2.$ Calculating the limits of the numerator and denominator separately, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)+2g(x))=6+2(2)=10\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(1+xg(x))=1+2(2)=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{f(x)+2g(x)}{1+xg(x)}=\\frac{10}{5}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23567a86-8e1e-5b94-bac6-09c79cdb42c9" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae6f200f-ddfc-5978-9499-832c6010156c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae6f200f-ddfc-5978-9499-832c6010156c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521155} :answer-field/id #uuid "696cc972-ece8-5b53-8faf-f9002d4c084b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bacd5fdb-8e88-5e6c-8872-876eba96fadd" :question/math-academy-id "q-300738" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b563936f37e82c3f6a422f80b448a7902a656a2749a7abced6c7bb9225ecf2bc.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -4}(2{x}^{2}+1)f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -4}f(x)=2.$ So, applying the product rule, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}(2{x}^{2}+1)f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}(2{x}^{2}+1)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -4}f(x) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-4)^{2}+1)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =66\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
